WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.874 --> 00:00:10.127 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to the Asia capitals, episode one of seven. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:27.200 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nine capitals run from the steppes of Central Asia to the Caucasus: Astana,
Ulaanbaatar, Bishkek, Tashkent, Dushanbe and Ashgabat, then Tbilisi, Baku and
Yerevan.

3
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.202 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Astana into view.

4
00:00:34.088 --> 00:00:44.363 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Astana stands on the Ishim river in the north of Kazakhstan, on the middle of
the Eurasian steppe, where the country chose its capital in 1997.

5
00:00:45.021 --> 00:00:55.772 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name means "capital" in Kazakh, and it has changed its name four times
since the Soviet era. It is one of the coldest capitals in the world.

6
00:00:56.425 --> 00:01:05.376 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Astana as the steppe capital renamed four times, with its Bayterek
tower meant to be a tree holding a golden egg.

7
00:01:06.054 --> 00:01:09.200 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ulaanbaatar into view.

8
00:01:12.085 --> 00:01:19.968 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ulaanbaatar stands in the valley of the Tuul river in the north of Mongolia,
where the winter is severe on the open steppe.

9
00:01:20.624 --> 00:01:28.252 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
"Red hero", a name given in 1924, it is the coldest national capital in the
world by average temperature.

10
00:01:28.913 --> 00:01:36.668 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Ulaanbaatar as the Red Hero, a moving monastery town that settled
here only in 1778.

11
00:01:37.352 --> 00:01:39.604 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bishkek into view.

12
00:01:43.406 --> 00:01:51.034 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bishkek lies at the foot of the Tian Shan mountains in the north of
Kyrgyzstan, with the range rising to its south.

13
00:01:51.694 --> 00:02:05.603 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From 1926 to 1991 it was called Frunze, after a Red Army commander born here,
and a statue of Manas, hero of the Kyrgyz epic, stands on its main square.

14
00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:13.168 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Bishkek as the city of the Tian Shan, with Manas, the epic hero, on
its main square.

15
00:02:13.852 --> 00:02:16.104 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tashkent into view.

16
00:02:20.047 --> 00:02:27.512 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tashkent lies in the fertile Fergana valley in the east of Uzbekistan, the
largest city of former Soviet Central Asia.

17
00:02:28.164 --> 00:02:40.146 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
"Stone city", much of it was rebuilt after an earthquake in 1966, and its
metro, opened in 1977, was the first in Central Asia.

18
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:50.017 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Tashkent as the stone city that rebuilt itself after the earthquake
of 1966, with Central Asia's first underground.

19
00:02:50.685 --> 00:02:53.320 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Dushanbe into view.

20
00:02:56.847 --> 00:03:03.116 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dushanbe stands in the valley of the Varzob river in the west of Tajikistan,
at the foot of the Pamirs.

21
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:14.438 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
"Monday" in Tajik, for the market held here on that day, it was a village
until 1924 and grew as the capital of Soviet Tajikistan.

22
00:03:15.101 --> 00:03:22.694 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Dushanbe as the Monday market town that became a capital in 1924,
once home to the world's tallest flagpole.

23
00:03:23.382 --> 00:03:26.273 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Ashgabat into view.

24
00:03:29.591 --> 00:03:38.124 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ashgabat lies at the edge of the Karakum desert in the south of Turkmenistan,
below the Kopet Dag mountains.

25
00:03:38.777 --> 00:03:49.992 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name is usually read as "city of love", from Persian. An earthquake
flattened it in 1948, killing a large part of its people.

26
00:03:50.647 --> 00:04:00.156 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Ashgabat as the city of love on the edge of the Karakum desert,
rebuilt after the earthquake of 1948.

27
00:04:00.897 --> 00:04:03.532 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Tbilisi into view.

28
00:04:12.736 --> 00:04:20.793 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tbilisi stands in a valley in the east of Georgia, on the Mtkvari river
between the ranges of the Caucasus.

29
00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:31.989 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
From tbili, "warm", for its sulphur springs. By legend King Vakhtang founded
it after his falcon fell into a hot spring while hunting.

30
00:04:32.643 --> 00:04:42.105 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Tbilisi as the warm sulphur spring of the legend, where the old town
mixes Georgian churches, a mosque and Persian bathhouses.

31
00:04:42.784 --> 00:04:44.618 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Baku into view.

32
00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:56.812 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Baku stands on the Caspian shore in the east of Azerbaijan, on the Absheron
peninsula.

33
00:04:57.469 --> 00:05:08.173 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name may come from a Persian phrase for "wind-pounded city", and at about
28 m below sea level it is the lowest capital in the world.

34
00:05:08.831 --> 00:05:15.867 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Baku as the lowest capital on Earth, its walled old city and Maiden
Tower a UNESCO site.

35
00:05:16.544 --> 00:05:18.541 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yerevan into view.

36
00:05:23.384 --> 00:05:30.118 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yerevan stands on the Hrazdan river in the west of Armenia, in the plain below
Mount Ararat.

37
00:05:30.777 --> 00:05:41.400 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It began as the fortress of Erebuni in 782 BC, 29 years before Rome by the
traditional date, and much of it is built of pink volcanic stone.

38
00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:51.312 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Yerevan as the pink city, founded as a fortress of Erebuni before
Rome, with the Matenadaran's medieval manuscripts.

39
00:05:56.536 --> 00:06:09.156 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Astana and Ulaanbaatar are the two great steppe capitals, with Bishkek,
Tashkent, Dushanbe and Ashgabat between them. Tbilisi, Baku and Yerevan close
the group in the Caucasus.

40
00:06:09.822 --> 00:06:27.786 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the capital renamed four times, the Red Hero of the cold, the stone
city rebuilt after its earthquake, the Monday market town, the city of love,
the warm spring, the lowest capital on Earth, and the pink city founded before
Rome.
